#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:40
msgid "Tools & data"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:75
msgid "In this section"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:159
msgid ""
"Analyzes your uploads and optimizes new ones as they arrive: it resizes "
"oversized uploads and recompresses them, and serves the next-gen copies they "
"already have on the front-end with a fallback to the original."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:160
msgid ""
"The \"Heaviest images\" view of Image optimization turns the aggregate "
"numbers into a concrete, per-image to-do list: a table with a thumbnail, "
"dimensions, weight, a plain-language tag of what would help each image "
"(Oversized, No WebP, No AVIF — only ever suggesting formats your server can "
"actually create) and an estimated saving, with search, a format filter and "
"pagination. On very large libraries the scan is time-bounded, so the list "
"shows the worst offenders it reached."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:162
msgid ""
"Compatibility with other optimizers: WPOptimizing recognizes WebP/AVIF "
"copies created by plugins such as Imagify, LiteSpeed Cache, EWWW or "
"ShortPixel (both the photo.jpg.webp and photo.webp naming conventions), so "
"it never regenerates a copy that already exists and can serve theirs too. If "
"one of those plugins is active and already serving next-gen images, "
"WPOptimizing automatically steps back from serving to avoid a double "
"<picture>."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:163
msgid ""
"Shows the CSS and JavaScript actually loaded on your public pages, sampled "
"in the background, and lets you stop specific assets from loading — on the "
"whole site or only where you choose."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:164
msgid ""
"Test mode (free): before applying your rules to everyone, turn on test mode "
"to try them safely. While it is on, every unload rule takes effect only for "
"logged-in administrators, so your visitors keep every asset. Browse your key "
"pages while logged in to confirm they still look and work correctly, then "
"turn test mode off to apply the rules to all visitors. This lets you "
"validate an aggressive setup with zero risk to the public site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:165
msgid ""
"URL exceptions (free): list the pages that must always load every asset — "
"the checkout, a booking form, a landing page — and no unloading of any kind "
"(site-wide or per page) will run there. Enter one URL pattern per line, "
"matched against the path, and use * as a wildcard: /checkout protects any "
"URL containing that path, */thank-you/* protects every order-received page. "
"The \"Quick add\" buttons fill in this site's own pages — the WooCommerce "
"cart, checkout and account, the Easy Digital Downloads pages, the course and "
"lesson addresses, the pages with a contact or booking form — and only appear "
"for what is installed here. This is the surgical companion to broad "
"unloading: strip assets site-wide, but never on the pages that need them."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:166
msgid ""
"Quick Profiles apply a full recommended configuration (cleanup tasks plus "
"object cache, query deduplication and heartbeat) with a single click. "
"Individual cleanup options and backup retention are configured here."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:167
msgid ""
"Notification email and maintenance report: the notification address is where "
"WPOptimizing sends the periodic report; for agencies, point it at the "
"maintainer instead of the site owner. Network alerts go to the network "
"administrator instead. The maintenance report is a recurring email summary "
"you can set to daily, weekly, fortnightly or monthly (off by default): it "
"includes the Health Score and its trend, key database and media metrics, any "
"unusual table growth, and the scans and cleanups done since the last report. "
"Use \"Send a test now\" to check delivery without changing the schedule."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:169
msgid ""
"You can opt out at any time from the Settings tab. Your cleanup features "
"keep working, but sharing is strictly reciprocal: if your site does not "
"contribute, it does not receive the shared dataset either — the community "
"knowledge base only flows to sites that take part. With sharing off, "
"attribution falls back to what your own site captures locally."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:175
msgid ""
"Analyzes your uploads (free) and, with Pro, optimizes originals (backup-"
"first), generates WebP and AVIF copies, resizes oversized uploads, and "
"serves the next-gen copies on the front-end with a fallback to the original."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:177
msgid ""
"Generate a WebP copy on upload (Pro) makes the WebP copy for a new image "
"(and each of its sizes) the moment it is uploaded, so the front-end can "
"serve next-gen straight away without waiting for the bulk pass. It is logged "
"exactly like the bulk copies, so \"Remove copies\" still undoes it. AVIF "
"stays a deliberate bulk action because encoding it is far too slow to run on "
"every upload. Like the rest, it stands down when another image optimizer is "
"active."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:178
msgid ""
"Compatibility with other optimizers: WPOptimizing recognizes WebP/AVIF "
"copies created by plugins such as Imagify, LiteSpeed Cache, EWWW or "
"ShortPixel (both the photo.jpg.webp and photo.webp naming conventions), so "
"it never regenerates a copy that already exists and can serve theirs too. If "
"one of those plugins is active and already serving next-gen images, "
"WPOptimizing automatically steps back from serving to avoid a double "
"<picture>. \"Remove copies\" only ever deletes the copies WPOptimizing "
"itself generated."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:179
msgid ""
"Shows the CSS and JavaScript actually loaded on your public pages, sampled "
"in the background. You can stop specific assets from loading site-wide "
"(free); automatic, context-aware unloading driven by the knowledge base is a "
"Pro feature."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:180
msgid ""
"Test mode (free): before applying your rules to everyone, turn on test mode "
"to try them safely. While it is on, every unload rule — manual and automatic "
"— takes effect only for logged-in administrators, so your visitors keep "
"every asset. Browse your key pages while logged in to confirm they still "
"look and work correctly, then turn test mode off to apply the rules to all "
"visitors. This lets you validate an aggressive setup with zero risk to the "
"public site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:181
msgid ""
"URL exceptions (free): list the pages that must always load every asset — "
"the checkout, a booking form, a landing page — and no unloading of any kind "
"(manual, automatic or per-page) will run there. Enter one URL pattern per "
"line, matched against the path, and use * as a wildcard: /checkout protects "
"any URL containing that path, */thank-you/* protects every order-received "
"page. The \"Quick add\" buttons fill in this site's own pages — the "
"WooCommerce cart, checkout and account, the Easy Digital Downloads pages, "
"the course and lesson addresses, the pages with a contact or booking form — "
"and only appear for what is installed here. This is the surgical companion "
"to broad unloading: strip assets site-wide, but never on the pages that need "
"them."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:182
msgid ""
"Quick Profiles apply a full recommended configuration (cleanup tasks plus "
"object cache, query deduplication and heartbeat) with a single click. "
"Individual cleanup options, backup retention and license management are all "
"configured here."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:183
msgid ""
"Notification email and maintenance report: the notification address is where "
"WPOptimizing sends license notices and the periodic report; for agencies, "
"point it at the maintainer instead of the site owner. Network alerts go to "
"the network administrator instead. The maintenance report is a recurring "
"email summary you can set to daily, weekly, fortnightly or monthly (off by "
"default): it includes the Health Score and its trend, key database and media "
"metrics, any unusual table growth, and the scans and cleanups done since the "
"last report. Use \"Send a test now\" to check delivery without changing the "
"schedule."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:184
msgid ""
"Import / export: download the whole configuration as a JSON file and restore "
"it here or on another site — handy to replicate your setup across a fleet. "
"The export covers the settings for cleanup, scheduling, cache, media and "
"images, Smart Assets, backups and attribution. For safety it never includes "
"keys, passwords, the license, webhooks, the data-sharing consent, branding "
"or measured runtime state (scan results, baselines, cached sizes), so "
"importing onto another site only carries configuration. On import, existing "
"settings are overwritten and any unrecognized keys are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:185
msgid ""
"You can opt out at any time from the Settings tab. Your cleanup features "
"keep working, but sharing is strictly reciprocal: if your site does not "
"contribute, it does not receive the shared dataset either — not even with an "
"active license. A license unlocks every feature of the plugin; the community "
"knowledge base only flows to sites that take part. With sharing off, "
"attribution falls back to what your own site captures locally."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:195 includes/admin/trait-performance.php:143
msgid "Performance assistant"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:197
msgid ""
"A guided view of your server response time (TTFB) with one-click fixes. It "
"rates the server share of TTFB, scores it into the Optimization health, and "
"walks you through the levers that lower it — applying the ones WPOptimizing "
"can do itself and explaining the ones it cannot."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:198
msgid ""
"The Performance tab measures the server share of TTFB — the time WordPress "
"takes to start sending a page, excluding the visitor network. It samples "
"real front-end requests, averages them, and shows a level (good / "
"improvable / high) that also feeds the Optimization health score."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:199
msgid ""
"Below the diagnosis is a checklist of levers. For the ones WPOptimizing can "
"act on — the persistent object cache, query deduplication, autoload weight "
"(Smart Deactivation) and the Heartbeat — an Apply button performs the change "
"in one click, reusing the same safe, reversible actions as their own tabs. A "
"result notice confirms each change."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:200
msgid ""
"For the levers WPOptimizing cannot change it shows guidance instead of a "
"button: moving WP-Cron to a real system cron (needs wp-config.php and server "
"access), and the server or host itself (PHP version, OPcache, hosting "
"resources, a light theme). This is the honest case: when the database, cache "
"and autoload are all green and TTFB is still high, the remaining time is the "
"server, and the assistant says so rather than suggesting cleanups that would "
"not help."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:202
msgid ""
"Deep analysis is an optional, consented profiler: it has zero effect on your "
"site until you click \"Run profiling test\", then it profiles just the next "
"few front-end requests and disarms itself. It breaks the server response "
"time into phases (boot, init, routing, queries and render) and summarises "
"the request's database work, so you get unequivocal information about where "
"the time goes — which is especially useful when everything WPOptimizing "
"controls is already optimal."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:203
msgid ""
"It also points to the concrete culprit: during the test it times each hook, "
"and because plugins fire their own hooks inside init (woocommerce_init, "
"elementor/loaded and so on), the \"Where the time went\" list names the "
"responsible plugin instead of just the generic phase — so you know exactly "
"what to disable or review."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:204
msgid ""
"The deep analysis also flags slow SELECT queries that scan a large table "
"without a usable index — the classic cause of a sluggish database — "
"attributing each to the plugin that ran it and, when the query is simple "
"enough, showing the exact CREATE INDEX you could add. With a license, Apply "
"index creates it for you after asking, and Undo removes it again, checking "
"afterwards that it is really gone; without one, it only advises, so you can "
"review it with your developer first."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:205
msgid ""
"The full breakdown opens on its own report screen — use \"View report\" from "
"the assistant (running a test lands you there directly). It is a plain URL "
"you can bookmark, share or print."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:213
msgid ""
"Fetches your Google PageSpeed score and Core Web Vitals for the home page, "
"on demand. It sends only the public URL of your site to Google’s public API "
"and changes nothing here. The optional free API key simply raises the "
"request quota."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:215
msgid ""
"PageSpeed Insights is Google’s own measurement of how fast your site loads. "
"WPOptimizing asks for it on demand — one outbound HTTPS request carrying "
"only the public URL of your home page — and shows the performance score plus "
"the Core Web Vitals (LCP, INP, CLS, FCP, TBT and Speed Index). Nothing is "
"installed, changed or stored on your site beyond the last result."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:216
msgid ""
"Lab metrics come from a simulated load and are always available. Field data "
"(what real visitors experienced over the last 28 days) is only included when "
"Google has enough traffic for your site, which is why it can be missing on "
"smaller sites. Test both mobile and desktop: mobile is the stricter of the "
"two and the one Google ranks on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:217
msgid ""
"The API key is optional. Without one Google applies a shared, anonymous "
"quota that is enough for occasional tests; if you test often you will "
"eventually see a quota error. A free key raises that limit, costs nothing "
"and needs no payment method."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:224
msgid ""
"Installs a persistent object cache drop-in that stores expensive database "
"queries in memory (Redis, Memcached, APCu or built-in SQLite). Query "
"deduplication collapses repeated identical queries within a single request. "
"Heartbeat control reduces admin-side server load."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:225
msgid ""
"WPOptimizing installs a drop-in file (wp-content/object-cache.php) that "
"intercepts WordPress object cache calls and routes them to the fastest "
"available backend. Redis and Memcached are used when the server offers them; "
"APCu works well on single-server hosts; the built-in SQLite store is the "
"automatic fallback that requires zero configuration."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:226
msgid ""
"Query deduplication (db.php drop-in) intercepts wpdb calls and returns a "
"cached result when the same query string is executed more than once in the "
"same HTTP request. This is particularly effective on complex pages that call "
"get_post(), get_terms() or get_user() for the same IDs from many different "
"code paths."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:227
msgid ""
"Login-safe by design: WordPress stores user session tokens inside the "
"user_meta cache group, so under heavy concurrent traffic a persistent cache "
"can occasionally overwrite a freshly issued login token and log users out. "
"WPOptimizing keeps the user_meta group out of the persistent store by "
"default and reads it fresh on every request, so sessions stay reliable. "
"Sites on a low-contention backend can opt back in from the cache config file."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:228
msgid ""
"Heartbeat control lets you set the WordPress admin heartbeat to Default, "
"Slow (every 60 s) or Off outside the editor, where autosave and edit locks "
"keep working. Slowing it or turning it off noticeably lowers PHP and "
"database load during active admin sessions."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:229
msgid ""
"Server TTFB and what to do about it: the Cache analytics show the server "
"share of TTFB (the time WordPress takes to start sending the page, not the "
"visitor network). WPOptimizing rates it (good / improvable / high), folds it "
"into the Optimization health score, and gives prioritized actions. The "
"advice is context-aware: if the object cache is off it suggests enabling it; "
"if autoload is heavy it points you there; and — crucially — when the "
"database, cache and autoload are already optimal it tells you honestly that "
"the bottleneck is the server itself (PHP version, OPcache, hosting resources "
"or a heavy theme/plugin), which the plugin cannot fix."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:230
msgid ""
"Page cache (opt-in, off by default): WPOptimizing can also serve a static "
"HTML copy of each page to anonymous visitors, so most requests never boot "
"WordPress — the biggest speed win. It stays disabled until you enable it in "
"the Cache tab, and it never caches logged-in users, carts, checkout, admin, "
"feeds, search, REST/AJAX or requests with a non-tracking query string; the "
"cache is purged automatically whenever content changes. Leave it off and "
"WPOptimizing only caches database queries and objects, which can never serve "
"a stale page or break a nonce."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:231
msgid ""
"Coexistence with host and plugin caches: WPOptimizing refuses to enable its "
"own page cache alongside a third-party one (WP Rocket, LiteSpeed, W3 Total "
"Cache…) or a host page cache, and it never overwrites another plugin’s "
"advanced-cache.php. It detects them and purges their page cache "
"automatically after a cleanup, and from the toolbar “Flush all caches”, so a "
"database cleanup never leaves stale pages. The Cache tab lists the page "
"caches it detected; a wpoptimizing_flush_all action lets hosts without a "
"known API hook their own purge."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:232
msgid ""
"Browser cache and compression (Apache): from the Cache tab WPOptimizing can "
"add a managed block to your .htaccess that sets far-future Expires/Cache-"
"Control headers for static files (images, CSS, JS, fonts) and enables on-the-"
"fly compression (Brotli when the server offers it, otherwise gzip). "
"Returning visitors then reuse files from their own browser instead of re-"
"downloading them, and every text response travels compressed. The block is "
"written between clearly marked WPOptimizing markers, a timestamped backup "
"of .htaccess is kept, and disabling it removes exactly that block and "
"nothing else. It is skipped when the server is not Apache or when another "
"plugin already manages these headers."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:233
msgid ""
"CSS and JavaScript minification (opt-in, off by default): under Speed › Page "
"& browser › Minification WPOptimizing can serve minified copies of your own "
"local CSS and JS, stripping comments and whitespace so they download faster. "
"It is deliberately conservative: files that are already minified, hosted on "
"another domain, or listed in your exclusions are left untouched; the "
"minified copies live in wp-content/uploads/wpopt-assets/ and are rebuilt "
"automatically when the source changes or a cache is flushed. A test mode "
"lets administrators preview the result before exposing it to visitors, and — "
"like the page cache — it refuses to run alongside another asset optimizer "
"(Autoptimize, WP Rocket, Perfmatters, LiteSpeed) to avoid double-processing."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:235
msgid ""
"Advanced CSS/JS delivery (Pro): on top of the free minification, Speed › "
"Page & browser can defer local JavaScript (scripts run after the page "
"paints, while jQuery stays eager so inline snippets keep working), load CSS "
"asynchronously (the loadCSS pattern, so stylesheets no longer block the "
"first paint), and inline your critical above-the-fold CSS in the head to "
"remove the brief flash the async loading would otherwise cause. Each has its "
"own exclusion list, and all of them stand down when another optimizer is "
"active."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:236
msgid ""
"Plugins by URL (Pro): under Speed › Page & browser › Plugins by URL you can "
"keep a plugin from loading on the URLs you choose, or load it only on them, "
"so its PHP, queries, CSS and JavaScript stay out of pages where it does "
"nothing. It applies to visitors only — logged-in users, the admin, AJAX, the "
"REST API and cron always get every plugin. Before saving, each set of rules "
"is tried on the home page and up to four more pages, loaded with every "
"plugin and with the rules; it is not saved if any of them breaks, nor if it "
"would leave out a plugin another one needs. If a visit ever ends in a fatal "
"error with plugins left out, every rule switches itself off. Deactivating "
"WPOptimizing undoes it all."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:238
msgid ""
"Cloudflare: if the site is behind Cloudflare, WPOptimizing also purges its "
"cache — everything when it flushes the other caches, and only the affected "
"pages when content changes. It needs the zone ID and an API token with the "
"Zone › Cache Purge › Purge permission; “How to get them” in the Cache tab "
"shows the steps. The Global API Key is refused, because it opens the whole "
"account. A token for “All zones” can be reused on every site of the account, "
"and it can only purge."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:239
msgid ""
"Pages at Cloudflare's edge (opt-in): with the page cache on and a token that "
"also has Zone › Cache Rules › Edit, WPOptimizing can add a Cache Rule so "
"Cloudflare keeps the pages too, for anonymous visitors only. Each page says "
"whether it may be kept — the pages the page cache stores, for as long as it "
"keeps them — and logged-in users, carts, searches, 404s and pages that set a "
"cookie always reach your server. Turning it on is checked with real "
"requests, and if a request with a session cookie or the login page is ever "
"served from the edge, the rule is removed by itself."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:245
msgid ""
"Autoloaded options are loaded from the database on every single page "
"request. This tab lists them sorted by size so you can disable autoload for "
"options that are not needed on every request, reducing the upfront database "
"cost on every page."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:246
msgid ""
"WordPress loads all autoloaded options in a single SELECT at the start of "
"every request. If plugins have added large blobs or configuration arrays "
"with autoload=yes, this query can transfer hundreds of kilobytes before a "
"single line of your template runs. The Autoload tab lists every autoloaded "
"option with its size, so you can identify the heaviest ones at a glance."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:247
msgid ""
"Smart Deactivation analyses every autoloaded option against the attribution "
"manifest and automatically disables autoload for options that are "
"confidently attributed to plugins that are no longer installed. This is "
"completely safe: an uninstalled plugin cannot need its options on every "
"request."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:248
msgid ""
"De-autoload never-read goes further and is the most surgical option. "
"WPOptimizing measures which of the largest autoloaded options are actually "
"read on each request (a sampled, near-zero-overhead measurement) and, "
"combined with the same signal aggregated across the network, turns off "
"autoload only for options that load on every request yet are (almost) never "
"read. WordPress core options are always protected, nothing is deleted, and "
"every change is reversible."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:249
msgid ""
"Disable all non-essential is the broadest reversible action: it de-autoloads "
"every option that is neither core nor owned by an installed extension "
"(options the network reads on most requests are kept). Delete confirmed "
"orphans removes, backup-first, the dead options of uninstalled extensions."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:250
msgid ""
"The Candidates sub-tab shows non-autoloaded options that are read on almost "
"every request (measured by the read-frequency tracker). Enabling autoload "
"for those options turns the per-read SELECT into a zero-cost cache hit."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:251
msgid ""
"Tip: a healthy site keeps total autoload weight below about 800 KB. Sites "
"above 1 MB will see a measurable latency improvement after a cleanup."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:257
msgid ""
"Lists every database table with its engine, row count, data size and "
"recoverable overhead. Optimizing a table reclaims fragmented space. Tables "
"still using the legacy MyISAM engine are highlighted for conversion to "
"InnoDB."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:258
msgid ""
"Database tables accumulate fragmented space over time as rows are deleted or "
"updated. The \"overhead\" column shows how much space can be reclaimed "
"without touching any data: MySQL simply compacts the existing rows. For busy "
"e-commerce or membership sites this can save tens or hundreds of megabytes."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:259
msgid ""
"When there is reclaimable overhead, a \"Reclaim overhead now\" button "
"appears above the list. It runs OPTIMIZE on exactly the tables that carry "
"recoverable overhead (file-based engines such as MyISAM and Aria) in one "
"click, so you do not have to find and select them by hand. It never touches "
"InnoDB internal free space, which the engine reuses on its own, and it never "
"deletes data."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:260
msgid ""
"MyISAM is the older MySQL table format. It lacks transaction support, uses "
"table-level locking (so a single slow query blocks all writes), and cannot "
"recover safely after a crash. InnoDB offers row-level locking, crash-safe "
"recovery via a redo log, and significantly better performance under "
"concurrent write workloads."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:261
msgid ""
"The conversion from MyISAM to InnoDB is an online ALTER TABLE that rebuilds "
"the table in place. It is non-destructive: all data is preserved, all "
"indexes are rebuilt, and WordPress continues to function normally "
"throughout. For safety, take a database backup before converting large "
"tables."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:262
msgid ""
"Orphaned custom tables from plugins that have been uninstalled appear in the "
"Orphans tab rather than here, so the Tables tab only lists tables that "
"belong to something still installed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:263
msgid ""
"After each scan WPOptimizing records the size and row count of every table "
"and compares them with the previous scan. When a table grows abnormally "
"between scans — both by a meaningful amount and by a large percentage — it "
"is flagged at the top of this tab (and in the performance report). This is "
"how you catch a runaway log table, a transient/cache table that never "
"clears, or a stuck background job before it bloats the database. A brand-new "
"table is reported as new, never as a warning."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:269
msgid ""
"Shows all WordPress scheduled events with their hook, next run time and "
"recurrence. Orphaned events — hooks whose callback function no longer "
"exists, usually left by uninstalled plugins — are flagged so you can remove "
"them safely."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:270
msgid ""
"WordPress Cron (wp-cron) fires scheduled tasks on the next page load after "
"the scheduled time. Each event is identified by its hook name. When a plugin "
"is uninstalled without cleaning up, its scheduled hooks remain in the cron "
"table but nothing ever runs them — they fire silently, waste a small amount "
"of server time, and clutter the schedule."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:271
msgid ""
"An event is flagged as orphaned only when TWO checks agree: nothing is "
"listening for its hook, AND the hook name does not belong to any plugin "
"still installed. The second check is not a refinement, it is what makes the "
"first usable: plugins such as MainWP Child, Complianz or LiteSpeed register "
"their cron callback only in the context where it runs — a REST call, the "
"front end, their own request — so on this admin screen the hook looks "
"unclaimed while the plugin is present and keeps re-creating the event. The "
"bias is deliberately conservative: when in doubt, not an orphan. Removing a "
"genuine orphan is entirely safe — no code listens for the hook, so firing it "
"has no effect, and removing it only stops the silent wasted trigger."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:272
msgid ""
"The list also shows legitimate recurring events (WordPress core, active "
"plugins) so you have a complete picture of what is scheduled and how "
"frequently. This helps spot events that are running too often or that you "
"did not expect."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:273
msgid ""
"The WP-Cron trigger panel at the top of the tab controls how cron fires. By "
"default WordPress runs it on visitor page loads, which adds latency to "
"random requests and can miss tasks on low-traffic sites. Disabling WP-Cron "
"writes the DISABLE_WP_CRON constant into wp-config.php (a protected backup "
"copy is kept in the plugin's data folder) and shows the exact crontab line "
"to add so a real server cron drives wp-cron.php on a fixed schedule. If wp-"
"config.php is not writable, the panel shows the snippet to add by hand. The "
"change is fully reversible from the same panel."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:279
msgid ""
"Optional speed features for the public site, configured in Speed › Page & "
"browser: speculative loading (prefetch/prerender the next page), content "
"image loading optimization, resource hints, self-hosted Google Fonts, CDN "
"URL rewriting, and a Server-Timing diagnostic header. All opt-in and "
"reversible; none cache full HTML pages."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:280
msgid ""
"Speculative loading uses the browser Speculation Rules API to load the next "
"page before the visitor clicks. In Conservative mode the next page is "
"prefetched when a link is clicked; in Eager mode it is prerendered on hover, "
"so navigation can feel instant. The admin, the login page, URLs with a query "
"string, and WooCommerce cart/checkout/account pages are excluded "
"automatically, as are links marked rel=\"nofollow\" or with the class no-"
"prerender / no-prefetch."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:281
msgid ""
"Content image loading optimization marks the first image of a post as the "
"priority (LCP) image — loaded eagerly with fetchpriority=high — while the "
"remaining images are lazy-loaded with asynchronous decoding. It uses the "
"WordPress HTML Tag Processor, so it works even on markup produced by page "
"builders."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:282
msgid ""
"The Server-Timing header is a diagnostic for developers: it reports the "
"WordPress processing time, the number of database queries and the object-"
"cache hit rate, visible in the browser DevTools Network panel. It is off by "
"default and intended for troubleshooting, not for end users."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:283
msgid ""
"Resource hints look at the external origins your page actually loads (web "
"fonts, CDNs) and ask the browser to resolve DNS and open the connection "
"early, so those assets start downloading sooner. WPOptimizing adds "
"preconnect for the well-known font origins and dns-prefetch for the rest, "
"automatically and only for origins the page really uses — there is nothing "
"to configure. The hints are advisory and harmless: a redundant one only "
"costs a cheap DNS lookup."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:284
msgid ""
"Preloading key resources goes one step further than a hint: it tells the "
"browser to start downloading a specific file right away, before it would "
"normally discover it. WPOptimizing preloads the featured image of the "
"current post or page (usually the largest above-the-fold image, so this "
"directly improves the LCP metric) with a high priority, and any extra URLs "
"you list — typically an above-the-fold web font or a hero image. The correct "
"type (image, font, style, script) is inferred from the file extension and "
"fonts get the required crossorigin attribute automatically. Preload "
"sparingly: it is a good default for the one or two resources the page needs "
"first, but preloading a long list forces the browser to fetch everything at "
"once and can make the page slower, not faster."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:285
msgid ""
"Self-hosting Google Fonts downloads the font stylesheet and its woff2 files "
"into your uploads folder and serves them from your own domain, so the "
"browser never connects to fonts.googleapis.com or fonts.gstatic.com. This "
"removes a third-party connection (faster first render) and addresses the "
"GDPR concern about Google Fonts leaking visitor IP addresses. Turn it on, "
"save, then open your site once so the fonts are detected and click "
"\"Localize now\": WPOptimizing fetches and rewrites them, and from then on "
"swaps the remote stylesheet for the local copy with no per-request network "
"calls. \"Remove local fonts\" reverts to the original Google-hosted fonts."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:286
msgid ""
"CDN rewriting changes the URLs of your static files (images, CSS, JS under "
"uploads, wp-content and wp-includes) to a CDN hostname, so visitors download "
"them from the CDN edge closest to them instead of your server. Enter the CDN "
"host (for example cdn.example.com), choose which folders to rewrite, and "
"save. Only local static files are rewritten — dynamic PHP endpoints and any "
"external URL are left untouched. First configure the CDN as a pull zone "
"whose origin is this site, and make sure it forwards the correct CORS "
"headers for fonts. WPOptimizing does not upload anything; it only rewrites "
"URLs, so switching the feature off instantly returns to serving from your "
"origin."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:287
msgid ""
"Compatibility with caching, SEO and image-optimization plugins: these "
"features are built to coexist. WPOptimizing never overwrites an object-"
"cache.php or db.php drop-in installed by another plugin — it detects it and "
"steps aside. Resource hints are de-duplicated against hints the theme or "
"another plugin already added. Image loading only ADDS attributes and skips "
"any image another lazy-loader has already deferred, so it does not fight a "
"dedicated image plugin or WordPress core. The sitemap and feed core-tweaks "
"simply switch off the built-in versions, which an SEO plugin normally "
"replaces anyway, so enabling them next to one is safe."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:288
msgid ""
"You do not have to configure these by hand: each cleanup profile (applied "
"from Settings or the welcome wizard) already enables sensible front-end "
"defaults, so choosing a profile sets them up for you. You can override any "
"of them anytime under Speed › Page & browser › Loading & delivery."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:294
msgid ""
"Optional toggles in Settings that disable WordPress features you may not use "
"(emojis, the version meta, RSD/WLW links, jQuery Migrate, oEmbed extras, "
"REST API head links and more), trimming front-end weight and head noise. All "
"are off by default and reversible."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:295
msgid ""
"WordPress loads a number of features on every page that many sites never "
"use: the emoji script and its DNS prefetch, a generator meta tag with your "
"version, RSD and Windows Live Writer links, a shortlink tag, the jQuery "
"Migrate compatibility script, oEmbed discovery and the wp-embed script, REST "
"API discovery links, and more. Each one is small, but together they add "
"markup, requests and a little attack surface. The Core tweaks section lets "
"you switch off the ones you do not need."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:296
msgid ""
"Tweaks are grouped by risk so you can decide how far to go. Safe tweaks have "
"no visible effect (they only remove head noise). Moderate tweaks may affect "
"a few setups — for example removing jQuery Migrate can reveal an old theme "
"or plugin that still relies on deprecated jQuery, and removing front-end "
"Dashicons assumes your theme does not use that icon font for logged-out "
"visitors."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:297
msgid ""
"Everything here is fully reversible: a tweak only removes a core hook or "
"adds a short-circuit filter while it is enabled, and unticking it restores "
"the default behaviour on the next page load. No content or settings are "
"deleted. Features that WPOptimizing already manages elsewhere — Heartbeat, "
"post revisions and autosave, the oEmbed cache, the object cache, query "
"deduplication and front-end asset unloading — are intentionally not "
"duplicated here; configure those on their own screens. Security-oriented "
"switches such as XML-RPC are also out of scope and belong to dedicated "
"hardening tools."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:298
msgid ""
"You do not have to enable these one by one: each cleanup profile (applied "
"from Settings or the welcome wizard) already turns on a sensible set — all "
"the Safe tweaks for every profile, plus selected Moderate ones for the blog "
"and aggressive presets."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:305
msgid ""
"The analysis is free and read-only: it walks /uploads and reports the total "
"image weight, a breakdown by format, how many images are large (≥ 300 KB), "
"how many exceed your max dimensions, and how many lack a WebP/AVIF copy, "
"with an estimate of the space a conversion could save. It never changes a "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:308
msgid ""
"Optimize originals (Pro) shrinks JPEG/PNG files in place and resizes any "
"that exceed your max width/height — without a visible loss of quality. It is "
"strictly backup-first: each original is copied to a backup before being "
"overwritten, the result is kept only if it is actually smaller, and each "
"image is processed once. \"Restore originals\" copies the backups back, so "
"the whole step is reversible. Run it before generating copies so the copies "
"are made from the optimized originals."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:309
msgid ""
"Generate next-gen copies (Pro) creates a .webp and/or .avif copy next to "
"each image (for example photo.jpg.webp). The originals are never modified or "
"deleted, and every copy we create is logged so \"Remove copies\" deletes "
"only our files, never another optimizer's. AVIF needs a newer server (PHP "
"8.1+ GD with AVIF, or Imagick with libheif); where it is unavailable, WebP "
"is still offered. Because the copies are derivatives, they do not appear in "
"the images-by-format table — they are listed in their own \"Generated "
"copies\" table."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:312
msgid ""
"Front-end serving wraps each image in a <picture> element with the AVIF and "
"WebP copies as sources and the original as the fallback. The browser picks "
"the first format it supports; browsers that support neither receive the "
"untouched JPEG/PNG. A source is only added when its copy actually exists on "
"disk, so serving is always safe."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:314
msgid ""
"Settings: Conversion quality (40–100) is the lossy knob for WebP, AVIF and "
"JPEG; PNG recompression is always lossless. Max dimensions set separate "
"width and height limits (0 on an axis disables it). Lossless mode keeps PNG "
"lossless and leaves JPEGs untouched unless they must be resized, since there "
"is no lossless JPEG recompression. Strip metadata removes EXIF/ICC/comments "
"for smaller files (turn it off to keep camera data; note that the GD "
"fallback always drops metadata). Optimize on upload applies the same "
"settings to new uploads: it resizes those above the max size (WordPress' "
"native handling, preserving the full-size original), recompresses them and "
"strips their metadata."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:321
msgid ""
"Every theme and plugin can enqueue its own CSS and JavaScript, and many load "
"their files on pages that do not use them — a slider script on your contact "
"page, a forms stylesheet site-wide. Smart Assets records which handles are "
"actually enqueued on real front-end page loads (it samples public visits in "
"the background; sharing must be enabled) and lists them so you can see what "
"each page carries."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:322
msgid ""
"Manual unloading (free): tick the assets you want to stop loading and save. "
"The selected handles are dequeued on the front-end site-wide, which removes "
"their HTTP requests and bytes. It is fully reversible — untick to restore. "
"Start conservatively and test the public site after each change, because "
"some scripts are dependencies of others."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:324
msgid ""
"Automatic unloading (Pro): instead of choosing by hand, WPOptimizing uses "
"the shared knowledge base to decide, per page type, which assets are safe to "
"drop — context-aware and kept up to date as the dataset grows. It is opt-in "
"and can be turned off at any time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:327
msgid ""
"Per-rule targeting (free): by default an unloaded asset is dropped on the "
"whole site. Under \"Per-rule targeting\" you can narrow any single rule to a "
"content type (front page, posts & pages, or archives) and/or to specific "
"URLs — the opposite of the exceptions: \"unload this ONLY on /shop and */"
"product/*\". Enter one or more URL patterns (comma-separated, wildcard *) in "
"\"Only on URLs\"; leave it empty to keep the rule site-wide. Scope and URL "
"are combined, so you can say \"on single posts, but only under /guides\". "
"Changes save as you edit."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:329
msgid ""
"Who owns an asset (free): your site can only name the handles its own "
"plugins print, so some rows show no owner at all — and an asset you cannot "
"name is one you cannot safely act on. WPOptimizing asks the shared knowledge "
"base: when the sites that contribute their registry agree on who owns a "
"handle, its owner is shown with a \"shared data\" mark, so you know the name "
"came from them and not from your own site. When they disagree — two "
"different extensions claiming the same handle — the row is marked "
"\"conflicting\" and no owner is shown. That is deliberate: we never pick the "
"most popular answer, because unloading an asset attributed to the wrong "
"extension breaks the page that needed it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:330
msgid ""
"Safety: unloading only stops an asset from being enqueued; it never deletes "
"files. If a page looks wrong after unloading something, re-enable it and the "
"asset loads again on the next request."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:338
msgid ""
"Removes database clutter: post revisions, auto-drafts, trashed posts, spam "
"comments, and expired transients. Runs from a single button or automatically "
"on a schedule. A detailed log is saved after every run."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:339
msgid ""
"Over time WordPress accumulates records that are no longer needed for the "
"site to function: every draft save creates a revision, every failed publish "
"leaves an auto-draft, deleted content sits in the trash, spam fills the "
"comments table, and transients created by plugins often expire but are never "
"purged. General Cleanup removes all of these in one pass."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:340
msgid ""
"Quick Profiles apply a recommended set of tasks in a single click. Every "
"profile enables the basic tasks (auto-drafts, trash, spam, expired "
"transients and table optimization); Safe, Blog and Membership add nothing "
"riskier, WooCommerce adds orphaned elements, Action Scheduler, WooCommerce "
"sessions and orphaned variations, and Agency (maximum) adds unapproved "
"comments, duplicate metadata, orphaned elements, Action Scheduler and unused "
"tags. No profile deletes all transients."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:341
msgid ""
"The Dry Run option shows a count of what would be removed without deleting "
"anything — useful for auditing before your first cleanup. After a real run, "
"a detailed HTML log is saved and accessible from the history list so you can "
"review exactly what was deleted."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:342
msgid ""
"Every batch the cleanup deletes is backed up first, into one Backups entry "
"per run (scheduled and automatic runs included). If that backup cannot be "
"written, the run stops with the status “Stopped: backup failed” and deletes "
"nothing more. Table optimization changes no data and needs no backup."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:343
msgid ""
"Tip: schedule cleanup to run nightly or weekly from the Settings tab. "
"Keeping the database lean reduces backup sizes and speeds up queries that "
"scan post meta or comment tables."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:344
msgid ""
"Per-post protection: open any post in the editor (block or classic) and tick "
"\"Keep all revisions\" to exclude that single post from revision cleanup — "
"useful for legal pages or contracts where the full edit history matters. "
"WPOptimizing also honors the keep_revisions custom field used by \"Optimize "
"Database after Deleting Revisions\", so you can migrate from that plugin "
"without reconfiguring anything."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:345
msgid ""
"The \"By content type\" sub-tab is a read-only diagnostic that shows where "
"your database weight concentrates, grouped by post type: live entries, "
"revisions, auto-drafts and attached metadata rows. Revisions and auto-drafts "
"are attributed to their real type (revisions through their parent post), so "
"you can see at a glance which content types carry the most overhead before "
"deciding what to clean."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:346
msgid ""
"Toolbar shortcuts: the WPOptimizing menu in the top admin bar runs a general "
"cleanup or a full analysis in one click from any screen, on desktop and "
"mobile alike."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:347
msgid ""
"Very large databases: the analysis runs asynchronously in stages, so it "
"never blocks the browser or times out a request. On installs with tens of "
"millions of rows, WPOptimizing automatically switches the heaviest counts to "
"fast approximate queries and caps the per-query time, so some figures (like "
"per-type metadata totals) may be shown as approximate or omitted to protect "
"the live site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:353
msgid ""
"Scans your uploads for files with no database attachment record, orphaned "
"thumbnail sizes, and exact-content duplicates. Duplicate detection keeps the "
"oldest copy and reassigns all post references before any deletion."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:354
msgid ""
"The Media tab covers three separate problem types. Potentially-unused "
"attachments are Media Library entries that are not referenced in any post "
"content, post meta, widget, option or theme mod — they may have been "
"uploaded and then forgotten. Review each one carefully: a file can be "
"\"unused\" in the database but still referenced by hard-coded HTML in a "
"template."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:355
msgid ""
"Duplicate detection computes a hash of the actual file content (not the "
"filename). When two or more files share the same hash, WPOptimizing keeps "
"the attachment with the oldest ID (the original) and reassigns every "
"post_content, post_meta and thumbnail reference that pointed to a duplicate "
"before deleting the duplicate file and its attachment record."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:356
msgid ""
"Orphaned thumbnail files are physical image files in wp-content/uploads that "
"correspond to intermediate sizes no longer registered by any active theme or "
"plugin. They consume disk space but are never served. They are safe to "
"remove once you have confirmed the size is no longer needed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:360
msgid ""
"Every media deletion is backed up first, so it is reversible: WPOptimizing "
"copies each file (and reassigns duplicate references) before removing "
"anything. If you ever need to undo a deletion, the Media tab shows how many "
"file backups are available and links straight to the Backups tab, where you "
"restore a whole operation in one click. Nothing is deleted that was not "
"fully backed up."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:361
msgid ""
"Image optimization (recompression, WebP/AVIF generation, resizing and front-"
"end serving) has its own sub-section, documented separately under Image "
"optimization."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:362
msgid ""
"In the Library audit, the Renamed-original backups group lists a specific "
"kind of leftover: the *-mfrh-original* copies that rename plugins such as "
"Media File Renamer keep as a backup of the pre-rename original and register "
"as the attachment original_image. The renamed file is what the site serves, "
"so these copies are redundant and do not appear under Orphan files (they are "
"still referenced). Deleting one is backup-first and also clears the leftover "
"original_image reference so nothing is left dangling."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:363
msgid ""
"Media has six entries: Orphans (with a filter for orphan files, orphan "
"thumbnails and other files), Duplicate sets, Unused attachments, Image "
"optimization (Summary and Heaviest images), Trash and Library audit. The "
"audit examines the uploads folder and offers a fix for each finding it can "
"stand behind: protect or delete files that should not be downloadable, "
"delete unused orphan sizes and loose files (backup first), regenerate "
"thumbnails that carry an older name, and a direct link when another Media "
"section already lists the file. It never moves files."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:369
msgid ""
"Records and files that could not be attributed to any known plugin or theme. "
"They may belong to a custom plugin not yet in the manifest, or to bespoke "
"code. Review them manually before taking any action."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:370
msgid ""
"Attribution uses a shared community dataset built from scan results "
"contributed by thousands of WordPress installations. When WPOptimizing finds "
"a database record or file that does not match any entry in the dataset and "
"cannot be inferred from the slug or prefix of an installed extension, it is "
"listed under “To identify” instead of being classified as Orphan or Inactive."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:371
msgid ""
"Every attributed item carries a confidence level, from most to least "
"reliable: Manual (you attributed it yourself), Sure, Likely, Possible, "
"Doubtful and Estimated; hover over a badge to see what it means. Only "
"Manual, Sure, Likely and Possible allow bulk deletion. Items to identify "
"that have no owner at all show Undetermined."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:372
msgid ""
"What to do with items to identify: first, check whether the record or file "
"belongs to a custom plugin or active feature of your site. If it does, no "
"action is needed. If you are confident it is orphaned clutter from something "
"you removed, you can delete it via the standard checkbox interface — a "
"backup is created first as with all deletions."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:373
msgid ""
"Attribute manually: when you know who owns an item to identify, the "
"\"Attribute manually\" panel above the list lets you teach WPOptimizing so "
"the item leaves “To identify”. Open its own help — the ? next to the panel "
"title — for the full guide."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:374
msgid ""
"The dataset grows with each contribution. Items still to identify today may "
"be automatically attributed after the next dataset update."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:380
msgid ""
"Every database record whose stored value is empty, with a Status column "
"telling you whether each one is To identify, Orphan, Inactive or In use. A "
"convenient way to clear leftover keys that hold nothing."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:381
msgid ""
"This section lists options and post/user/term meta whose value is empty: "
"NULL, an empty string, or an empty serialized/JSON structure (a:0:{}, [], "
"{}). It deliberately ignores values like 0 or false, which carry meaning. A "
"meta key appears only when EVERY record under it is empty, never when only "
"some are; the “Empty records” column then shows the total — all of them "
"empty."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:382
msgid ""
"The Status column reuses the same attribution engine as the rest of the "
"plugin, so each row is marked To identify (no known owner), Orphan (owner "
"not installed), Inactive (owner installed but off) or In use (owner running "
"now). This lets you clean with judgement instead of blindly."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:383
msgid ""
"Safety: records owned by an active extension or by WordPress core are shown "
"read-only — they have no checkbox and cannot be deleted here, because a key "
"may be empty on purpose and removing it could break the owner. Only empties "
"marked To identify, Orphan or Inactive can be selected."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:384
msgid ""
"Empty-row deletion is backup-first and removes only the empty rows for a "
"key, never the non-empty ones that share the same name — so it is fully "
"restorable from the Backups tab."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:390
msgid ""
"Manually tell WPOptimizing who owns items it could not attribute. Matched "
"items leave “To identify” and move to Orphan or Inactive depending on "
"whether the owner is installed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:391
msgid ""
"Use this panel when you know who owns an item to identify but the shared "
"dataset does not. Add a rule that matches either by “contains” — a family of "
"items that share a fragment, e.g. wysija → MailPoet — or by “exact”, a "
"single specific value. Rules are applied at display time, so matched items "
"leave “To identify” on the next load without re-scanning, and are re-"
"classified as Orphan (owner not installed) or Inactive (owner installed but "
"currently off)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:392
msgid ""
"Pick one or more owners per rule. The owner box searches as you type across "
"three sources: your installed plugins and themes, the public wordpress.org "
"repository, and the shared knowledge base — so you can pin the exact "
"extension even if it is no longer installed, or type a slug for one that is "
"not listed. Choosing several owners at once (e.g. MailPoet and MailPoet "
"Premium) creates one rule per owner, which is useful when a value belongs to "
"a plugin and its add-ons."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:393
msgid ""
"Faster still: tick the rows you recognise in the “To identify” list, click "
"“Attribute selected”, and choose the owner(s) once — WPOptimizing creates "
"one exact rule per selected row and owner and opens this panel with them "
"prefilled."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:394
msgid ""
"If the owner you pick is currently active, WPOptimizing also refuses to "
"delete the matched items — including items already ticked in a list that was "
"loaded before you added the rule. An owner that is switched off or no longer "
"installed does not protect anything: attributing an orphan is how you get to "
"delete it knowingly."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:395
msgid ""
"The rules you have added are listed under the form with their own search, "
"owner filter and pagination; click “Remove” to delete a rule. Removing a "
"rule sends its matched items back to “To identify” on the next load."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:396
msgid ""
"With your consent (the shared-dataset setting), each rule is also sent — "
"batched and off-request, never blocking the page — to the shared knowledge "
"base, where it is verified before being promoted. Your manual hints improve "
"automatic attribution for everyone over time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:402
msgid ""
"Ask the shared knowledge base to suggest the owner of database records still "
"to identify. After each analysis the sure ones are applied on their own; you "
"confirm the rest, and nothing is ever deleted automatically."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:403
msgid ""
"The assistant appears under Analysis results › To identify › Database and "
"under Orphans › Database. It sends only the identifier NAMES of the records "
"shown (option, meta, cron and table names — never their values or any "
"content) to the shared knowledge base, which returns the most likely owning "
"plugin or theme for each one. On Orphans it also compares the answer with "
"the owner already on screen and flags the records where the two disagree, "
"which is what you want to see before deleting anything."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:404
msgid ""
"Each result carries a verdict: Sure or Likely (a confident, namespace-backed "
"match), Possible (a single-owner hint to confirm), or Ambiguous / No data "
"(shown for review only, with no owner). Confident results offer an Accept "
"button that creates an exact manual attribution rule — exactly as if you had "
"typed it yourself — so the record leaves “To identify” on the next load and "
"your choice also feeds the knowledge base. The assistant is retrieval over "
"the shared knowledge base, not a language model: it can only answer with "
"owners that are already recorded there, and it abstains instead of guessing. "
"When there are several Sure results, “Accept all sure suggestions” accepts "
"them in one go."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:405
msgid ""
"Only the Sure verdicts are applied on their own: after each analysis, in the "
"background, the records still to identify are asked about, and the Sure "
"answers become Likely attributions — below anything this site saw itself, "
"never manual rules, and they never delete anything. Everything else waits "
"for you. A record attributed to a plugin that is not installed becomes an "
"orphan like any other, deleted only when you choose to and with a backup "
"first. The assistant is opt-in: it requires the shared-dataset setting to be "
"on, since it relies on the same anonymous knowledge base you contribute to."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:411
msgid ""
"Records and files confirmed as belonging to plugins or themes that are no "
"longer installed. The manifest attributes them with high certainty, so "
"nothing on the site uses them. Delete them unless you plan to reinstall that "
"extension and want to keep its settings; orphan deletions are backed up "
"automatically beforehand."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:412
msgid ""
"Items are listed as Orphans when the dataset can attribute them to a "
"specific extension slug AND that slug is not present in the list of "
"installed plugins or themes. Its confidence must be Possible or higher "
"(Manual, Sure, Likely or Possible) for the item to be classified as an "
"Orphan; with Doubtful or Estimated it stays in “To identify”."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:413
msgid ""
"Database orphans include options (rows in wp_options), custom tables, user "
"roles, post meta keys, taxonomies, and custom post type records left by "
"plugins that were deleted without running their uninstall routines. File "
"orphans are files and directories inside wp-content that belonged to a "
"plugin or theme folder that is now gone."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:414
msgid ""
"Orphan deletion is backup-first: WPOptimizing exports the affected rows (for "
"database items) or records the file paths (for filesystem items) into a "
"restorable backup entry before removing anything. If you delete something by "
"mistake, go to the Backups tab and restore the relevant operation."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:415
msgid ""
"Tip: use the extension filter above the table to delete all orphans from a "
"single plugin at once, rather than selecting rows one by one."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:416
msgid ""
"Protected items: you can mark any database element as protected so it is "
"never deleted, even by a \"delete all\" action, regardless of how the "
"attribution heuristics classify it. Click the shield icon next to an item in "
"the To identify, Orphans, Inactive or Empty lists to protect it (the shield "
"turns green) or click it again to unprotect. The shield is not shown under "
"In use: those records belong to currently-active extensions and are never a "
"deletion target, so there is nothing to protect them from. To protect (or "
"unprotect) many at once, tick the rows and use the single \"Protect "
"selected\" button: it unprotects when every ticked row is already protected, "
"and protects otherwise (leaving any already-protected rows untouched). This "
"is a hard safety net for the rare case where an item is misattributed. "
"Developers can protect items programmatically by returning false from the "
"wpoptimizing_can_delete_item filter."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:422
msgid ""
"Database records and files belonging to plugins or themes that are installed "
"but currently inactive. These items are not needed while the extension is "
"off, but may be required again if it is reactivated. The Plugins & Themes "
"sub-tab lets you uninstall inactive extensions directly."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:423
msgid ""
"Inactive items are a middle ground: the plugin is still installed (its files "
"are present), but it is not active, so its database entries and any files it "
"manages outside its own plugin folder are serving no purpose right now. "
"Whether to delete them depends on your intent: if the plugin is being kept "
"around for occasional use, leave its data alone; if you know you will never "
"reactivate it, uninstall it properly."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:424
msgid ""
"The Plugins & Themes sub-tab lists every inactive plugin and theme with a "
"checkbox for selection. Clicking Delete runs the plugin's own uninstall hook "
"(if it has one) and removes its files. The \"Also remove associated data\" "
"checkbox cascade-deletes the database and filesystem items associated with "
"those slugs from the Inactive lists at the same time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:425
msgid ""
"Caution: deactivating a plugin does not remove its data. Uninstalling it "
"may, depending on whether the plugin's author implemented an uninstall "
"routine. WPOptimizing's cascade-delete fills that gap for items it can "
"attribute, but it cannot know about data a plugin stores in non-standard "
"locations."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:431
msgid ""
"Read-only view of records and files attributed to currently active plugins "
"or themes. No deletion is allowed here. It is an audit view so you can see "
"what active extensions are storing and how much space they use."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:432
msgid ""
"The In use list (Analysis results › In use) gives you a complete picture of "
"what each running plugin or theme is storing in your database and "
"filesystem. Because the extension is active, deleting these items would "
"break functionality — the tab is intentionally read-only. Its purpose is "
"transparency and accountability."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:433
msgid ""
"Use the extension filter to drill down into a single plugin and see every "
"option, post meta key, custom table or file it owns. This is useful when "
"auditing a new plugin before committing to it, or when investigating "
"unexpected database growth."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:434
msgid ""
"Each item carries its confidence level (Manual, Sure, Likely, Possible, "
"Doubtful, Estimated or Undetermined), which says how its owner was "
"established: from your own rule, captured live, read from the code or the "
"shared dataset, or guessed from a prefix. Here it matters only for "
"correcting a wrong owner or protecting a record, since nothing in this list "
"is deleted."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:442
msgid ""
"Tracks your site's database size, autoload weight, table overhead and other "
"metrics over time. A snapshot is recorded automatically after every scan and "
"cleanup so you can measure the impact of each action."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:443
msgid ""
"Every time a scan completes or a cleanup runs, WPOptimizing captures a "
"snapshot of key metrics: total database size, autoloaded options weight, "
"total table overhead, number of orphaned records, and more. These snapshots "
"build a timeline you can browse to see whether your site is getting leaner "
"over time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:444
msgid ""
"The headline cards at the top summarise the metrics that matter most — "
"including a Server TTFB card with a trend sparkline — each showing the "
"current value, the change since the baseline, and a mini chart of its "
"evolution. A card always shows the latest recorded value of its metric, even "
"if the most recent snapshot happened to be a database-only scan that did not "
"capture it, so the same set of cards appears consistently."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:445
msgid ""
"The baseline is the very first snapshot recorded. All subsequent values are "
"shown relative to it so you can see absolute improvements at a glance. You "
"can reset the baseline at any time — for example, after a major cleanup — to "
"start measuring from a new reference point."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:446
msgid ""
"The Export button streams the full timeline as a CSV file. Import it into a "
"spreadsheet to build your own charts, share it with a client, or keep it as "
"an audit record."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:447
msgid ""
"Browse the timeline with the event and date filters, per-page and search — "
"the same controls as the other tables. You decide how long snapshots are "
"kept with the \"Keep timeline data\" control below the timeline (Forever, 3 "
"months by default, 6 months or 1 year); older ones are pruned automatically "
"each day, while the pinned baseline is always kept."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:453
msgid ""
"A change journal for the whole site: what other plugins and themes add or "
"remove over time — tables, options, post/user meta keys, scheduled events "
"and top-level files — each dated and attributed when possible."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:454
msgid ""
"When WPOptimizing is installed it captures a baseline signature of your "
"site: the set of database tables, option names, post and user meta keys, "
"scheduled cron hooks, and the top-level entries under plugins, themes, mu-"
"plugins and uploads. From then on it compares the current state against that "
"baseline at meaningful moments and records what changed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:455
msgid ""
"A change is captured whenever a plugin or theme is installed, activated, "
"deactivated, deleted or updated, and after each analysis. This has no per-"
"page-load cost — nothing is measured on normal requests. When the change "
"rides a plugin or theme action, that extension is recorded as responsible, "
"so you can see exactly what a newly installed plugin added to your database "
"and filesystem."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:456
msgid ""
"Each entry shows the date, whether the item was added or removed, its type, "
"its name, and the responsible extension (or Unknown for background changes). "
"Filter by type or change and search by name, with the same controls as the "
"other tables. The journal is capped and kept out of autoload, so it never "
"weighs on the site; you can clear it at any time and tracking continues from "
"the current baseline."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:462
msgid ""
"Every change WPOptimizing makes to your data is backed up first: deletions "
"(orphans, empty rows, media, the general cleanup and its scheduled and "
"automatic runs) and changes (autoload flags, scheduled tasks, .htaccess and "
"wp-config.php edits). Backups are grouped by operation so you can find and "
"restore exactly what a specific action changed. Manual backups can also be "
"created at any time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:463
msgid ""
"Before WPOptimizing removes or changes database rows, it serialises them "
"into a backup file under wp-content/wpoptimizing/backups/ (not in the "
"database). For filesystem deletions it writes a manifest and copies each "
"file alongside it; an edited file (.htaccess, wp-config.php) is copied "
"whole. Each entry is tagged with the operation that created it (orphan "
"deletion, database cleanup, autoload change, media deletion, etc.) and a "
"timestamp, so you can quickly find the one you need. If the backup cannot be "
"written — disk full, permissions, or the size limit set in Settings — the "
"action does not run."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:464
msgid ""
"To restore a backup, open the Backups tab, find the entry, and click "
"Restore. For database backups this re-inserts the rows keeping their "
"original IDs; for file backups it copies the files back to their original "
"paths and reports any it could not restore."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:465
msgid ""
"Old backups are pruned automatically according to the maximum count and "
"maximum age limits set in Settings. You can also delete individual backups "
"manually once you are confident the cleanup was correct. Creating a manual "
"backup from the Backups tab captures the current state of a table or option "
"set you specify."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:466
msgid ""
"Clean uninstall: by default, deleting the plugin from the Plugins screen "
"removes every trace it created — all settings and transients, scheduled "
"tasks, the object-cache and database drop-ins, the runtime mu-plugin, every "
"backup and scan file, and the WebP/AVIF copies it generated. Your already-"
"optimized images are kept (only the extra next-gen copies and the backups "
"are removed; originals are not restored). You can turn this off in Settings "
"if you plan to reinstall and want to keep your configuration and backups. "
"Note that deactivating the plugin never deletes anything — only deleting it "
"does."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:473
msgid ""
"Quick Profiles are the fastest way to configure WPOptimizing for your site "
"type. Each profile bundles a curated set of cleanup rules together with the "
"appropriate performance settings: Safe, Blog and Membership stick to the "
"basic tasks; WooCommerce adds the store-specific ones (sessions, orphaned "
"variations, Action Scheduler); Agency (maximum) adds duplicate metadata and "
"unused tags as well."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:474
msgid ""
"Applying a profile also configures the Cache tab automatically: the "
"appropriate cache backend is installed, query deduplication is enabled or "
"disabled based on the profile's recommendation, and the heartbeat rate is "
"set. You can override any of these from the Cache tab after applying a "
"profile."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:475
msgid ""
"Backup retention: set a maximum number of backup entries, a maximum age in "
"days and a maximum total size. Entries beyond the count or age limit are "
"pruned automatically; when a new backup would cross the size limit, the "
"action stops instead of filling the disk. The minimum retention is 1 entry "
"and 1 day — WPOptimizing always keeps at least the most recent backup."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:478
msgid ""
"License: enter your license key here to activate Pro features (deletion of "
"attributed records, unlimited scan rows, media deduplication). The key is "
"validated against the wpoptimizing.com API. Internet access is required for "
"license checks; they are rate-limited to once per hour."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:486
msgid ""
"Send a JSON POST to an external URL whenever WPOptimizing finishes a scan, a "
"cleanup, a deletion or a dataset upload. Compatible with Slack, Zapier, n8n "
"and any custom HTTP endpoint, and signed with HMAC-SHA256 when you set a "
"secret."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:487
msgid ""
"Webhooks let you integrate WPOptimizing events into your existing automation "
"workflows. There is one endpoint, set in Settings › Webhooks, and a POST "
"request goes to it whenever one of the events you selected occurs (with none "
"selected, all of them are sent). The payload is a JSON object with the "
"event, the site URL, the plugin version, a timestamp and the event data."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:488
msgid ""
"Every request carries an X-WPOptimizing-Event header with the event name. If "
"you set a signing secret (optional), it also carries X-WPOptimizing-"
"Signature: sha256= followed by the HMAC-SHA256 of the raw JSON body keyed "
"with that secret, so your endpoint can confirm the request is genuine and "
"was not altered in transit."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:489
msgid ""
"Events: scan_completed, cleanup_completed, items_deleted and "
"dataset_uploaded. items_deleted includes the names or paths of what was "
"deleted (up to 100 per request), so send it only to a system you trust."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:495
msgid ""
"WPOptimizing uses a community manifest that maps plugin and theme slugs to "
"their known database tables, options and files. The more sites that "
"contribute their scan results, the more accurate attribution becomes for "
"everyone."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:496
msgid ""
"Attribution accuracy is the core quality metric of WPOptimizing: the more "
"precisely the plugin can say \"this option belongs to plugin X\", the safer "
"and more useful the cleanup is. The shared dataset is a crowd-sourced "
"manifest that grows with every participating site. When your site uploads "
"its scan results, the new data is merged into the dataset and made available "
"to every participating site on the next update."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:497
msgid ""
"Dataset uploads never include page URLs, post content, user data, option "
"values or personal information. The upload contains only structural "
"metadata: table names, option key patterns, file path patterns, and the "
"plugin slug they are associated with, plus your site’s domain, which "
"authorises the exchange."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:502
msgid "Languages and custom translations"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:504
msgid ""
"English plus eight complete translations. If you want to change a string, "
"put your catalogue in wp-content/languages/plugins/ — never inside the "
"plugin folder, where an update would delete it and where it would not be "
"read."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:505
msgid ""
"The plugin ships English as its source language and eight complete "
"translations: Spanish (es_ES), German (de_DE), French (fr_FR), Italian "
"(it_IT), Portuguese for Portugal (pt_PT), Russian (ru_RU), Arabic (ar) and "
"Chinese Simplified (zh_CN). Portuguese is pt_PT and is not the same as "
"Brazilian Portuguese (pt_BR); a pt_BR site falls back to English until "
"someone contributes that locale."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:506
msgid ""
"Catalogues are shipped in the WordPress 6.5 performance format (.l10n.php), "
"which loads faster than the classic .mo and is the format WordPress prefers. "
"The .pot template ships too, which is what a translation tool needs to start "
"a new locale or to send us a correction."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:507
msgid "Where to put your own catalogue:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:508
msgid ""
"wp-content/languages/plugins/, named wpoptimizing-<locale>.mo "
"(or .l10n.php). WordPress searches that folder BEFORE the plugin's own, so "
"your file wins, and it survives plugin updates — anything inside the plugin "
"folder is replaced on every update."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:509
msgid "The one trap worth knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:510
msgid ""
"do not drop your .mo inside the plugin's own languages/ folder. Since "
"WordPress 6.5 the .l10n.php in the SAME folder is preferred over a .mo, so "
"your file would sit there being ignored with no error to tell you. In wp-"
"content/languages/plugins/ there is no .l10n.php of ours to shadow it, which "
"is exactly why that is the right place."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:511
msgid ""
"Loco Translate and similar tools already save to that folder by default, so "
"following their defaults is correct. If you improve a translation, send it "
"back and it ships for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: includes/admin/trait-docs.php:531
